Exact commands for you
•	To apply the migration to your configured Azure SQL database (explicit command you should run locally; do NOT run automatically from startup):
•	cd C:\Training\NovaAI\src\NovaBanking.Infrastructure
•	dotnet ef database update --project ./ --startup-project ../NovaBanking.Api
Notes:
•	Ensure your Api project configuration (appsettings / user secrets) contains the correct ConnectionStrings:NovaBanking pointing to your Azure SQL database before running the command.
•	The --startup-project points to the project that has the configured DI/Configuration (NovaBanking.Api).
•	To run the idempotent seed against your configured Azure SQL database (opt-in):
•	From the solution root run:
•	dotnet run --project src/NovaBanking.Api -- --seed
•	This runs the Api in seed mode which will execute DbSeeder.SeedAsync against the configured NovaBankingDbContext and then exit.
Notes, caveats, and recommendations
•	The InitialCreate migration already existed and matched the authoritative model snapshot. No migration duplication was performed.
•	The seeder intentionally does not call context.Database.Migrate() or EnsureCreated(). Apply migrations explicitly as shown above.
•	The --seed startup mode runs seeding once and exits; do not run it against production or shared databases unless intended and approved.
•	The in-memory tests use Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory pre-release packages; review package versions if you plan to stabilize to GA releases.
•	I removed the explicit diagnostic connection.OpenAsync from /health to avoid unintended side effects and potential secret/logging risks. /health now does a safe CanConnectAsync and logs a simple message.
